[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
ns drafts, it mints the Done-when part's own drafts instead - the whole single-kind verdict or its one ## done-when section - and refuses repeated Dispositions inside that valid section or one supplying no draft. A sitting with no or repeated done-when section, or a DONE-WHEN line under another heading, is invalid, covers nothing, and leaves the uncovered arm to mint the goalposts row.\"\"\""," rationale = \"\"\"Every merge is judged from records - the verdict files, the Done-when checklist - and each of them could be changed by the lane being judged: a reviewer committing work beside its verdict, a later session rewriting an earlier round, a builder rewording its own Done-when. Nothing on a commit reliably names its role, but the coordinator's session log does, so the checks key on it and are re-derived at merge from what was committed rather than trusted from the loop. Reading a round as its session committed it removes the rewrite without adding a refusal. The Done-when claim rule warns rather than refuses because the intake mint still files its derived rows without a Done-when, and a refusal would park every one of them.\"\"\""," status = \"Approved\""," component = \"CMP-008\""]},{"oldStart":2851,"oldLines":8,"newStart":2851,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-178\", \"SR-228\"]"," title = \"An adjudication re-attestation constrained at merge\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/acceptance_record.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py\"","-code_symbol = \"first_approval_scope/adjudication_approval_refusal/merge_approval_refusal/_registry_identity/_reattested_registries/amendment_scope/reattested_between/_wrote_ledger/verdict_rulings/_tier_of/_below_approval/_held_row_line/_held_act_lines/held_reattest_refusal/reattest_scope_refusal\"","-detail = \"acceptance_record.merge_approval_refusal calls reattest_scope_refusal and held_reattest_refusal for an adjudication lane, and they act only when the merge delta wrote the act ledger. reattested_between reads the ledger at the merge base and the head and takes the ids re-attested by the entries whose seq the base does not hold, refusing by name when either side does not parse. Every such id outside amendment_scope - the union of the claimed amendment rows' own amendment scope, including their amendment:<id> tokens when a combined sitting claims them, empty when none is claimed - is refused by name before the first-approval judgement runs. For each re-attested row whose tier the dial at trunk's tip holds - trunk's tip being the commit the merge lands on, not the merge base - held_reattest_refusal refuses the row by name when it is below approval or absent at the head, when its act names no verdict, when its named verdict is not accepted - bound, recorded accepted and valid - or when verdict_rulings reads that accepted verdict's amendment part and does not rule the row CLARITY; a row tagged both MEANING and CLARITY reads MEANING. An act that both approves rows and re-attests rows, claimed either by two single-kind rows or by one combined sitting's per-kind tokens, merges when each half lies inside its claiming row's own kind scope, whether their rows are in one registry or different ones; an id under another combined kind authorizes neither half, a snapshot is WIDENED only when the act neither flipped nor re-attested a row in that registry identity: every carrier path of an authorized registry, including a deleted obsolete carrier copy, is authorized, while an unauthorized registry's copy is WIDENED on every carrier path, and every held re-attestation has its CLARITY verdict.\"","+code_symbol = \"first_approval_scope/adjudication_approval_refusal/merge_approval_refusal/_registry_identity/_reattested_registries/amendment_scope/reattested_between/_wrote_ledger/_added_acts/verdict_rulings/_tier_of/_below_approval/_held_row_line/_held_act_lines/held_reattest_refusal/reattest_scope_refusal\"","+detail = \"acceptance_record.merge_approval_refusal calls reattest_scope_refusal and held_reattest_refusal for an adjudication lane, and they act only when the merge delta wrote the act ledger. reattested_between reads the ledger at the merge base and the head and takes the ids re-attested by the entries whose seq the base does not hold, refusing by name when either side does not parse. Every such id outside amendment_scope - the union of the claimed amendment rows' own amendment scope, including their amendment:<id> tokens when a combined sitting claims them, empty when none is claimed - is refused by name before the first-approval judgement runs. For each re-attested row whose tier the dial at trunk's tip holds - trunk's tip being the commit the merge lands on, not the merge base - held_reattest_refusal refuses the row by name when it is below approval or absent at the head, when its act names no verdict, when its named verdict is not accepted - bound, recorded accepted and valid - or when verdict_rulings reads that accepted verdict's amendment part and does not rule the row CLARITY; a row tagged both MEANING and CLARITY reads MEANING. Every act the merge adds that names a verdict, on a held or released rung and including a first approval in the same act, must name an accepted verdict; acts already on trunk are not re-read, and an act naming no verdict is judged by the other scope rules alone. An act that both approves rows and re-attests rows, claimed either by two single-kind rows or by one combined sitting's per-kind tokens, merges when each half lies inside its claiming row's own kind scope, whether their rows are in one registry or different ones; an id under another combined kind authorizes neither half, a snapshot is WIDENED only when the act neither flipped nor re-attested a row in that registry identity: every carrier path of an authorized registry, including a deleted obsolete carrier copy, is authorized, while an unauthorized registry's copy is WIDENED on every carrier path, and every held re-attestation has its CLARITY verdict.\""," rationale = \"\"\"At merge the first-approval scope check read flips alone, and a re-attestation moves no cell, so an amendment act could re-anchor rows its verdict never ruled. The act ledger is the one record naming the rows each act re-attested, which makes the scope checkable where the merge is judged; a lane claiming no amendment has ruled no amended text, so it may re-anchor none.\"\"\""," status = \"Approved\""," component = \"CMP-006\""]},{"oldStart":3233,"oldLines":7,"newStart":3233,"newLines":7,"lines":[" title = \"Coordinator adjudication entry attributes a current verdict to its claimed lane call\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/coordinator_adjudicate.py\""," code_symbol = \"DEFAULT_ROUTE/resolve_route/lane_refusal/reserve_verdict/bind_requested/_requested/_release/adjudicate/signin/main\"","-detail = \"\"\"ENTRY. coordinator_adjudicate runs only from a claimed lane worktree and otherwise refuses before launch through IF-284. It also refuses an unusable route or unreadable or undecodable brief through that interface. As its last pre-launch checks it creates the verdict path's missing parent directories and then the path itself, empty and exclusively, and creates its requested-kinds binding beside it exclusively, so an existing or concurrently reserved path or binding, or one whose parent cannot be created, also refuses; a pre-launch refusal removes the empty reservation and binding and leaves any directory it created. Otherwise it composes the shared request, plans the keep, launches and records an ADJUDICATE call with coordinator attribution, then records accepted only for a valid in-deadline successful verdict or failed for every other launched outcome and commits that binding in its own telemetry commit, and prints the IF-285 reading. A non-zero exit or timeout returns the failed-call outcome through IF-284 without reading the verdict; after a successful in-deadline call, an empty reservation is a missing verdict recorded failed. A launched call leaves its verdict file, empty or written, for inspection. The default route and every command outcome are declared by IF-283 and IF-284, and the command also exposes a sign-in reading.\"\"\"","+detail = \"\"\"ENTRY. coordinator_adjudicate runs only from a claimed lane worktree and otherwise refuses before launch through IF-284. It also refuses an unusable route or unreadable or undecodable brief through that interface. As its last pre-launch checks it creates the verdict path's missing parent directories and then the path itself, empty and exclusively, and creates its requested-kinds binding beside it exclusively, so an existing or concurrently reserved path or binding, or one whose parent cannot be created, also refuses; a pre-launch refusal removes the empty reservation and binding and leaves any directory it created. Otherwise it composes the shared request, plans the keep, launches and records an ADJUDICATE call with coordinator attribution, then validates the verdict and records accepted only when that validation and an exit-0, in-deadline call both hold, otherwise failed, and commits that binding in its own telemetry commit, and prints the IF-285 reading. A non-zero exit or timeout returns the failed-call outcome through IF-284 without reading the verdict; after a successful in-deadline call, an empty reservation is a missing verdict recorded failed. A launched call leaves its verdict file, empty or written, for inspection. The default route and every command outcome are declared by IF-283 and IF-284, and the command also exposes a sign-in reading.\"\"\""," rationale = \"This one entry point serves SR-227's coordinator-route and sign-in obligations as well as SR-231's lane-local admission and evidence-freshness boundary: a call outside its claimed lane could commit telemetry on trunk, and a path not exclusively reserved for this call could carry an earlier call's verdict. The entry point therefore owns admission and evidence freshness, while IF-283, IF-284 and IF-285 each own the command contract they expose; copying those contracts here was rejected because two homes for the same route or exit condition would drift.\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-321)\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":3280,"oldLines":8,"newStart":3280,"newLines":8,"lines":[" sr_refs = [\"SR-232\"]"," title = \"Combined sitting composes and validates kind-scoped adjudications\""," module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/adjudicate_brief.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/sitting.py;project-trajectory/scripts/coordinator_adjudicate.py\"","-code_symbol = \"requested_kinds/_sitting_scopes/combined_values;scopes/sections/own_section/brief_scope/GRAMMAR/DONE_WHEN_OUTCOMES/line_refusal/named_kinds/sitting_refusal/parse/requested_path/render_requested/read_requested/REQUESTED_SUFFIX/OUTCOMES/write_binding/accepted/accepted_part/accepted_at;bind_requested/_requested;record_outcome\"","-detail = \"A combined class accepts only amendment:<id>, first-approval:<id> and done-when:<id> Adjudicates tokens; it composes each named class through compose with that section's verdict path in fixed amendment, first-approval, done-when order, and refuses whole if any section cannot compose. One parser reads a verdict into its kind lines: a combined sitting names exactly the kinds bound at reservation in <verdict>.requested, has exactly one kind-form lower-case section per bound kind and none other, and has exactly one complete own-kind machine line in each section; a single-kind verdict has no kind-form section and exactly one complete machine line. The binding records pending at reservation and accepted or failed after a route runs the call; write_binding is its one writer and record_outcome its one route recorder and commit path. The coordinator verdict check, Done-when holds and held-rung re-attestation consume the one accepted-verdict reader, so only an accepted, bound, valid verdict counts; failed, pending, unbound or other-class verdicts cover nothing. Token and section parsing are shared by composition, validation, act scopes and merge-time minting; the class is routed and retainable through BRIEF_PROMPTS.\"","+code_symbol = \"requested_kinds/_sitting_scopes/combined_values;scopes/sections/own_section/brief_scope/GRAMMAR/DONE_WHEN_OUTCOMES/line_refusal/named_kinds/sitting_refusal/parse/requested_path/render_requested/read_requested/REQUESTED_SUFFIX/OUTCOMES/write_binding/accepted/accepted_part/accepted_at/unaccepted_refusal;bind_requested/_requested;record_outcome\"","+detail = \"A combined class accepts only amendment:<id>, first-approval:<id> and done-when:<id> Adjudicates tokens; it composes each named class through compose with that section's verdict path in fixed amendment, first-approval, done-when order, and refuses whole if any section cannot compose. One parser reads a verdict into its kind lines: a combined sitting names exactly the kinds bound at reservation in <verdict>.requested, has exactly one kind-form lower-case section per bound kind and none other, and has exactly one complete own-kind machine line in each section; a single-kind verdict has no kind-form section and exactly one complete machine line. The binding records pending at reservation and accepted or failed after a route runs the call; write_binding is its one writer and record_outcome its one route recorder and commit path, recording accepted only when the call succeeded and its validation passes. The coordinator validates a pending binding before recording its outcome; later consumers use the accepted-verdict reader, which returns only an accepted, bound, valid verdict. Every added act naming a verdict likewise needs an accepted verdict; failed, pending, unbound or other-class verdicts cover nothing. Token and section parsing are shared by composition, validation, act scopes and merge-time minting; the class is routed and retainable through BRIEF_PROMPTS.\""," rationale = \"A shared checkpoint reduces adjudication launches only if no constituent judgement loses its scope or grammar. One parser prevents composition, validation and later acts from interpreting the same sitting differently.\""," test_refs = \"TC-327\""," status = \"Approved\""]}]},{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-841\\docs\\work\\active\\wi-841\\WI-841-done-when-blessed-in-lane.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":88,"oldLines":3,"newStart":88,"newLines":30,"lines":[" The first-approval sitting at 88314d5 (`007-ADJUDICATE-88314d5.md`) approved LLR-307, LLR-308, LLR-310, SR-232, TC-325 and TC-327 and returned TC-328, with one coverage gap on LLR-307, answered the same way:"," "," - TC-328's Method runs every test its Evidence cites; a test now drives LLR-307's retired-key clause and TC-326 cites it (`e80a0ddf`, `97c87102`).","+","+## Dispositions","+","+Verdict: `docs/reviews/wi-841-done-when-blessed-in-lane/013-ADJUDICATE-37217b4.md`.","+LLR-262, LLR-278, LLR-310 and TC-326 are MEANING and can be blessed. LLR-306","+is CLARITY. TC-278 is MEANING and is returned for its Method's wording. TC-326","+cannot be re-anchored until TC-278 is, because they share the test-case","+registry's snapshot copy.","+","+```toml","+title = \"Move TC-278's released-rung cases out of its held-rung clause\"","+workstream = \"process\"","+buildtier = \"low\"","+safety_class = \"spine\"","+sr_refs = [\"SR-178\", \"SR-228\"]","+priority = 8","+```","+","+TC-278 (returned): its Method lists \"a released-tier act naming an unaccepted","+verdict\" and \"a first approval in such an act\" inside the sentence opening","+\"With the rung held, ...\". The tests behind them","+(`test_a_released_tier_act_naming_an_unaccepted_verdict_is_refused`,","+`test_a_first_approval_in_an_act_naming_an_unaccepted_verdict_is_refused`)","+run on a RELEASED rung. Move the two cases into their own sentence, stating","+the released rung and that the whole act, its first approval included, is","+refused. Do not change the behaviour or the tests. Re-sit the row's amendment","+adjudication, then re-attest TC-278 and TC-326 together in one act."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-841\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\README.md","C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-841\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\acts.toml","C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-841\\docs\\archive\\last_approved\\docs\\requirements\\low-level-requirements.toml","C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-841\\docs\\work\\active\\wi-841\\WI-841-done-when-blessed-in-lane.md"]}}}
