{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfnSu3XDF5WYsVas4E1wy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Five of the six rows change meaning and one, LLR-306, is clarity only (`VERDICT: MEANING rows=6`). I re-attested the four LLR rows. I sent TC-278 back, which leaves TC-326 unanchored for now.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **LLR-262, LLR-310 and TC-326:** a verdict now counts as accepted only if its call also succeeded (exit 0, within the deadline) as well as validating. A loop that recorded a valid verdict from a failed call as accepted would fail the new text. TC-326 adds the two tests for this.\n- **LLR-278 and TC-278:** the accepted-verdict requirement now covers every act a merge adds that names a verdict, on released rungs too, including a first approval in the same act. Before, it covered held-rung re-attestations only.\n- **LLR-306 (clarity):** \"valid in-deadline successful verdict\" is now spelled out as \"validates, and the call exited 0 within the deadline\". The old text already sent a non-zero exit or timeout to \"failed\" without reading the verdict, so the obligation is the same.\n\n**Why TC-278 goes back:** its Method lists the two new cases (a released-tier act naming an unaccepted verdict, and a first approval in such an act) inside the sentence that starts \"With the rung held, …\". The tests behind them both run on a released rung, so read literally the Method asks for a fixture that can't exist. Its Expected states the rule correctly. I drafted the fix under the spec's `## Dispositions`: move those two cases into their own released-rung sentence, with no change to behaviour or tests.\n\n**Commits:**\n- `60dfbe69` — the verdict file `013-ADJUDICATE-37217b4.md` plus the TC-278 draft, with the trailer `WI: WI-841`.\n- `927a09eb` — the re-attestation of LLR-262, LLR-278, LLR-306 and LLR-310 (act seq 53).\n\nThe pre-commit checks passed on both commits.\n\n**Still open:**\n- **TC-326 is not re-attested.** I'd bless it, but it shares the test-case registry with TC-278. I ran the snapshot for TC-326 to confirm: it was refused, naming TC-278's Expected and Method, and wrote nothing. Once TC-278's Method is fixed and re-judged, re-attest both in one act.\n- **The binding file:** `013-ADJUDICATE-37217b4.md.requested` is still untracked. It belongs to the coordinator's outcome commit, so I left it alone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":982,"cache_read_input_tokens":357921,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":982},"output_tokens":761,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2cffbbc6-0175-4e90-b9ab-7b165e0c0246","uuid":"bd7ada55-b5d2-4f47-a204-464f9d337b0c","timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:01:55.629Z","request_id":"req_011CfnSu2pYdRi5S8wmtGfdw"}
